course: biology
lesson: 80
title: Animals of North America and Britain: moose, bald eagles, raccoons, beavers, foxes, badgers, hedgehogs, robins
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: North America and Britain share some animals, like the red fox, and have their own stars: the moose wading in a lake, the bald eagle on the Great Seal, the badger snuffling at dusk and the hedgehog on the garden lawn.
explain: Names can trick you: a bald eagle has a full head of white feathers, and the American robin is a different, bigger bird from the British robin, named by homesick settlers because its red breast reminded them of home.
explain: Many of these animals live right next to us: foxes trot down city streets at night, raccoons open bins, and robins follow gardeners for worms, just as their ancestors followed wild boar.
explain: Cartoons show raccoons as masked burglars washing their loot, hedgehogs carrying apples on their spines and skunks spraying for fun; the real animals are stranger and smarter, and a skunk always warns you first.
fact: The moose is the largest deer, up to about 2 metres tall at the shoulder; it dives several metres for salty water plants, grows and drops huge antlers every year, and can be dangerous, so it should be watched from far away.
fact: The bald eagle is not bald; its name comes from an old word meaning white, for its white head, and it became the national bird of the USA in 1782.
fact: Raccoons often dunk food in water even when it is clean, because wet, sensitive front paws seem to feel things better; they are not really washing it.
fact: Beaver front teeth are orange because the enamel contains iron, which makes it extra strong, they never stop growing, and beavers build dams so the entrance to their lodge stays under water and safe from predators on land.
fact: Red foxes live in many British towns and cities, eating worms, beetles, rats, fruit and scraps, and the eerie screams on winter nights are often foxes calling.
fact: Badgers live in families in tunnel systems called setts, some used for over a hundred years, and a badger can eat a few hundred earthworms in one night.
fact: A hedgehog has about 5,000 to 7,000 spines, which are hollow hairs, eats mostly insects, worms and beetles, hibernates from about November to March, and should be given water and meaty pet food, never bread and milk, because milk upsets their stomachs.
fact: The European robin sings all winter, both males and females defending territory with song, and its red breast warns rivals, so it may even attack a bunch of red feathers; it was voted Britain's favourite bird in 2015.
fact: The American robin is a thrush, bigger than the British robin, with a grey back and reddish breast, and it often finds worms by sight.
fact: Grey squirrels brought from North America in the 1800s took over much of the red squirrel's range in Britain; they carry squirrelpox, which kills many reds, and reds survive best in places like Scotland.
fact: A striped skunk sprays a stinking oily liquid a few metres from glands under its tail, usually after stamping and raising its tail as a warning.
fact: Millions of monarch butterflies fly up to about 4,000 kilometres from eastern North America to Mexico each autumn, using the sun and a body clock, though none of them has been there before.
fact: The monarchs' journey north takes several generations, so the butterflies returning to Mexico are the great-great-grandchildren of those that left.
exercise: What is the moose? | The largest kind of deer | A kind of wild cow | A large kind of goat | A small kind of elk
exercise: Why does a moose dive in summer lakes? | To reach water plants rich in salt and minerals | To catch fish by scooping them up in its wide flat antlers | To wash its antlers clean | To hide from summer rain
exercise: Where does the bald eagle's name come from? | An old word meaning white, for its white head | Its head has no feathers at all once it is fully grown | It loses all the feathers on its head every winter | It was named after an American explorer called Bald
exercise: When did the bald eagle become the national bird of the USA? | 1782 | 1972 | 1492 | 2007
exercise: Why does a raccoon dunk its food in water? | Wet paws seem to feel the food better | It is washing off the dirt like a person | It is cooling food that is far too hot to eat | It is drowning the food first so it stops
exercise: Why are beaver teeth orange? | The enamel contains iron, which makes it extra strong | Beavers eat lots and lots of carrots from the farmers' fields | The wood stains them orange | They are rusting from the water
exercise: Why does a beaver want its lodge doorway under water? | Predators on land cannot get in | It likes wet feet when it sleeps | Fish bring food to the door for it | It cannot climb out of the water
exercise: What makes the eerie screams on British winter nights? | Often foxes calling | Owls catching mice in the dark | Badgers fighting in their setts | Hedgehogs snoring under hedges
exercise: What is a badger's home called? | A sett | A drey in a tree | A lodge of sticks | A form in the grass
exercise: What do badgers mostly dig for at night? | Earthworms | Acorns | Fish | Bird eggs
exercise: What are a hedgehog's spines? | Hollow hairs | Sharp little bones | Long fingernails | Stiff feathers
exercise: What should you put out for a hedgehog? | Water and meaty cat or dog food | A bowl of bread soaked in cow's milk | Salted crisps | Apples on a plate
exercise: Why does a European robin sing all winter? | Males and females defend a feeding territory with song | They are calling for spring to come back | They are cold and singing keeps their little bodies warm at night | Only the babies sing in winter
exercise: What is the American robin? | A thrush, bigger than the British robin, named after it | The very same bird as the British robin, which flew over the sea | A kind of eagle with a red breast | A parrot from Canada
exercise: Why are red squirrels in trouble in Britain? | Grey squirrels took over their range and carry squirrelpox | They were hunted for their red fur to make hats and coats for the rich | They cannot climb pine trees | Foxes eat all the red ones
exercise: What does a skunk usually do before spraying? | Stamps its feet and raises its tail as a warning | Runs straight at the attacker's legs and bites them hard | Hides its head in a hole | Rolls into a spiky ball
exercise: How far do monarch butterflies fly south each autumn? | Up to about 4,000 kilometres | About 40 kilometres | About 40,000 kilometres | About 400 metres
exercise: How do monarch butterflies find their way to Mexico? | Using the sun and a body clock | Following butterflies that went before | Reading road signs | Smelling the Mexican flowers
test: «gonna feed the hedgehog bread and milk like in my picture book» What is right? | Milk upsets their stomachs; put out water and meaty pet food | Perfect, that is their favourite meal, just like in every picture book | Fine, as long as the milk is warm | Fine, but add some chocolate
test: In a cartoon, a hedgehog rolls through an orchard and carries apples on its spines. What is real? | Hedgehogs mostly eat insects, worms and beetles, not apples | Hedgehogs really do collect apples on their spines to store for winter | Hedgehogs eat only apples | Hedgehogs have no spines
test: «raccoons wash their food coz they're clean freaks» What is right? | They dunk even clean food, probably to feel it better with wet paws | Yes, raccoons wash everything twice and hate even a speck of dirt on it | Yes, raccoons hate any dirt | No, they never touch water
test: «bald eagles are bald, it's in the name, duh» What is right? | They have white head feathers; bald is an old word for white | Yes, they lose all their head feathers when they grow up | Yes, only the bald ones are called eagles | No, they have red head feathers
test: «the robin over in Canada's the same as ours, just bigger» What is right? | No, the American robin is a thrush, a different bird named after ours | Yes, exactly the same bird species, it just eats more over there | Yes, they swap countries every winter and come back fatter in spring | No, the American robin is an eagle
test: «a skunk's raising its tail at our tent, shut up and keep still or chase it?» What is the best answer? | Back away slowly and give it lots of room; that is a warning | Chase it off with a stick before it gets any closer to the tent | Run straight at it shouting | Try to pick it up gently
test: «monarch butterflies fly to Mexico and back, same ones» What is right? | The trip north takes several generations; the returners are their descendants | Yes, each one flies there and back twice | Yes, they live for twenty years, so they can make the trip again and again | No, they never leave Mexico
test: A monarch flies to a forest in Mexico it has never seen. What does that tell you? | The way is built into its body, not learned from older butterflies | It followed a map drawn by its parents | It followed the smell of its mother all the way from Canada to Mexico | It was carried by the wind by luck
test: What would happen if every beaver dam on a stream were removed? | The pond would drain and the animals living there would lose their home | The stream would make a bigger pond | Nothing at all would change | More beavers would move in
test: «squirrels are squirrels, who cares if grey ones took over» Why do people care? | Greys carry squirrelpox, which kills native red squirrels | Greys are much better for the woods because they plant more trees | Red squirrels are pests from America | Grey squirrels are endangered too
test: «moose looks chill, I'll get close for a photo» What is the best answer? | Watch from far away, moose can be dangerous if approached | Fine, moose are as tame as cows and like having their picture taken | Fine, if you offer it bread | Fine, they only charge in winter
test: «why's the robin attacking the red scarf on the washing line, mental» What is right? | Its red breast warns rivals, so it may attack red cloth as an intruder | It is hungry and trying to eat the scarf | Robins hate washing lines | It wants the scarf as a soft lining for the nest it is building nearby
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the derivation of the name of the bald eagle. | The term derives from an archaic word signifying white, for the head | The term refers to the absence of feathers upon the head of the adult | The term commemorates an individual of the surname Bald | The term refers to the annual moulting of all head feathers in winter
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the raccoon is observed to immerse its food in water. | Moistened forepaws appear to give a keener sense of touch | The raccoon thereby removes dirt in the manner of a human being | The raccoon thereby cools food of an excessive temperature | The raccoon thereby drowns living prey before consumption
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the American robin? | It is a thrush, larger than the European robin, to which it owes its name | It is identical in species to the European robin | It is a bird of prey with a reddish breast | It is a parrot native to Canada
exam: Ascertain the relationship between the monarch butterflies that depart southward and those that return to Mexico in the following autumn. | The returning butterflies are descendants of the departing ones, several generations on | The returning butterflies are the very same individuals | The returning butterflies are of a different species | No butterflies return to Mexico in the following autumn
